--TEST--
a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross `rule:classes/graph-copy`'s graph copy, and a `tainted` payload cannot come back through it — ADR 0033 § 4's `serialize()`-and-`spawn` bullet and ADR 0088 § 1's sink
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two halves of Stage 5's checker rule, which point in opposite
// directions across the same operation. `encode` refuses a `secret` argument
// because the bytes outlive the request and carry no qualifier; `decode`
// refuses a `tainted` one because the payload names classes and rebuilds their
// declared properties, so an attacker-chosen byte string picks which class the
// program is handed.
//
// The `encode` half is a call-site rule rather than a parameter type: the
// member declares `mixed`, which a `secret string` satisfies, so the written
// argument is the last place the qualifier is visible.
string $plain = "plain";
tainted string $t = "t";
secret string $s = "s";
secret bytes $sb = "s" as bytes;
secret tainted string $st = "s";

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. `tainted` is not on `encode`'s list:
// ADR 0033 § 4 names one qualifier at this sink, and taint says nothing about
// who may hold the bytes.
bytes $ok = Core\Serialize::encode($plain);
bytes $alsoOk = Core\Serialize::encode($t);
mixed $back = Core\Serialize::decode($ok);
echo $back;

// ADR 0033 § 1's `secret` on its own, at the operation ADR 0033 § 4 names.
bytes $one = Core\Serialize::encode($s);

// The other base, and the two bits composed — `secret` is one axis and
// `tainted` never launders it.
bytes $two = Core\Serialize::encode($sb);
bytes $three = Core\Serialize::encode($st);

// Composed rather than named: the qualifier poisons through concatenation on
// its own axis, so what is read at the call site is the argument's inferred
// type and not a `secret`-typed variable.
bytes $four = Core\Serialize::encode("bearer " . $s);

// ADR 0088 § 1's sink, in the other direction. `Core\Serialize::decode`'s
// parameter is declared `bytes` under `Qual::Sink`, so this one is an ordinary
// assignability refusal with no launderer in front of it — the way in is
// `Core\Json::decode` plus a checked conversion, not a cast.
mixed $five = Core\Serialize::decode($t as bytes);
echo $five;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Serialize::encode` writes it into bytes that outlive the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
   --> case.nvs:28:37
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Serialize::encode` writes it into bytes that outlive the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
   --> case.nvs:32:37
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Serialize::encode` writes it into bytes that outlive the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
   --> case.nvs:33:39
%A
error[E0775]: a `secret`-qualified value cannot cross a copy boundary; `Core\Serialize::encode` writes it into bytes that outlive the request, where nothing carries the qualifier that was protecting it
   --> case.nvs:38:38
%A
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
   --> case.nvs:44:38
